<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexander Jagiellon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1461">1461</a>–<a href="/years/1506">1506</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-dynasty">Jagiellonian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alexander Jagiellon was grand duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> from 1492 and king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> from 1501 until his death in 1506, a son of Casimir IV. He married Helena, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, but could not prevent Muscovite conquests, including the defeat at Vedrosha in 1500. In 1505 he accepted the Nihil novi constitution, which strengthened the Polish parliament.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-jagiellon.jpg" alt="Alexander Jagiellon" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan Goraj · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Goraj_Miracle_of_Saint_Simeon_Stylites_(detail).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alexander was born on 5 August 1461, the fourth son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV Jagiellon</a> and Elizabeth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, a daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-ii-of-germany">Albert II of Germany</a>. By his father's death in 1492 his eldest brother Vladislaus was already king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and, from 1490, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, and the next brother, Saint Casimir, had died in 1484 after an ascetic and pious life. On his deathbed Casimir IV recommended John Albert as his successor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and Alexander as ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> elected Alexander grand duke in July 1492 without consulting the Polish nobility, although the union required both sides to agree. John Albert was elected king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> on 27 August 1492 and claimed to be supreme duke over his brother, which Alexander recognised but the Lithuanian Council of Lords did not. Soon after his accession Grand Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-tatars">Crimean Tatars</a> attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. A peace of 1494 ceded extensive land to Ivan, and in 1495 Alexander married his daughter Helena in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>. Ivan resumed the war in 1500. After the Battle of Vedrosha, a truce of 25 March 1503 cost Lithuania about a third of its territory and 19 cities.</p><p>John Albert died on 17 June 1501, and Alexander was elected king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> on 3 October and crowned on 12 December. Short of money, he fell under the Polish Senate and nobility, who took the mint from him and curtailed his powers. In 1505 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> passed the Act of Nihil novi, which forbade the king to make laws without the consent of the nobility's two chambers, except for those governing royal cities, crown lands, mines, fiefdoms, royal peasants and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. Pope Julius II issued 29 bulls in Poland's favour and granted Peter's Pence, which helped Alexander restrain the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Alexander never felt at home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and favoured his fellow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a>, above all the magnate Michael Glinski, whose victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> at Kletsk on 5 August 1506 was reported to Alexander on his deathbed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>. He died on 19 August 1506. Interwar Lithuanian historians held that he understood but did not speak Lithuanian, though sources for this are lacking; his charters contain Ruthenian explanations of legal terms, and in 1504 he addressed a Tatar envoy in Ruthenian through an interpreter. His forgotten sarcophagus was found in Vilnius Cathedral in 1931.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alexander Jagiellon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.06,19.94&amp;year=1490" aria-label="Kraków on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5007 422 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alexander Jagiellon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5457 692h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5457 692h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.06,19.94&amp;year=1490">What was here in 1490? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
